## 2. Simple In-Memory Cache (Session-Only)

If you want an even simpler cache that only lasts while the page is open (no persistence), you can use an in-memory JavaScript object.

### Example:

```javascript
const weatherCache = new Map();
const CACHE_TTL = 60 * 60 * 1000; // 1 hour TTL

async function fetchWeather(city) {
  const key = city.toLowerCase();
  const cached = weatherCache.get(key);

  if (cached && (Date.now() - cached.timestamp < CACHE_TTL)) {
    console.log('Returning cached data for', city);
    return cached.data;
  }

  const apiKey = 'YOUR_API_KEY'; // Replace with your actual API key
  const url = `https://api.openweathermap.org/data/2.5/weather?q=${encodeURIComponent(city)}&units=metric&appid=${apiKey}`;

  try {
    const response = await fetch(url);
    if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`API error: ${response.status}`);
    const data = await response.json();

    weatherCache.set(key, { data, timestamp: Date.now() });
    return data;
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Error fetching weather data:', error);
    if (cached) {
      console.warn('Returning stale cached data due to fetch error');
      return cached.data;
    }
    throw error;
  }
}
```

### Benefits:
- Very fast access (in-memory).
- No storage size limits.
- Simple to implement.
- Cache cleared on page reload.

